A||N||5||Seanh||Passenger side wheel hard to move :(||||9||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-18-2001||02:09 PM||||Well, as I am working on my steering system, I am finding more and more problems! Anywho, for those that DON'T know about what happened yet (if there is anyone), we crashed the Fiero into the outside markers on a dirt track, (aka 7" Railroad ties on end out of the ground) and the front passenger tire took the impact of the collision all on its own. Now, the steering system is all removed and being replaced. However, I found today that, with the rims, rack, and everying off, the passenger side hub assembly (guess thats what its called?) is very difficult to turn (from left and right, not actually spinning). Now the drivers side is easy to turn, but the passenger is not nearly as easy. Visually, it doesn't look like anything is binding, nor like anything is obviously bent. What could be the problem? Perhaps just needs greasing? What can I do to fix this? Does it really need to be fixed, (the assey is turnable, but alot more difficult then the other side)? <P>I just wanna say thanks guys! for all the help you've given me, mostly on fixing the damage from this accident. I would be so broke, and so dead if it wasn't for this forum.||||reg||9
Z||000001||Steve Normington||06-18-2001||03:11 PM||||My thought is that one of the ball joints is busted/damaged. Try removing the upper and seeing how hard it is to turn the hub on the lower, then remove the lower, and see how easily the hub turns on the upper. I don't think that greasing would do anything.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadaver||06-18-2001||09:51 PM||||I too say a ball joint is hammered.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||06-18-2001||10:16 PM||||Excellent...New ones are on their way from Fiero Store tomarrow morning. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000004||starfighter007@msn.com||06-18-2001||10:28 PM||||I hope you have some one with you .I am not sure if you ever changed ball joints before .they are relitivly easy to do but can be dangerous at the same time.if your not sure let us know now  so we can give some hints to make job safer and positive||||reg||11
Z||000005||Seanh||06-19-2001||01:42 AM||||Nope, never messed with any ball joints before. If your thinking of releasing the sping to be the dangerous part, Iv done that before. Not on a Fiero though. I'm sure it tells the process in the Haynes manual, but if anyone would like to add anything to what it says about taking the spring out feel free to do so. I know that damn manual can conviently leave of some fairly important steps here and there. I was taking the rack and pinion off, and it said all I had to do was remove the coupling bolt and it should come off the steering shaft, if not, gently work it loose with a screwdriver. I beat, pried, pulled, kicked, yanked, jerked, and screamed at the thing all night, from 12 to 4am and still couldn't get it off, and finally went to sleep for the night. Posted on the forum and was told to put a screwdriver in the coulping ring. I did so, and taped it with a hammer 3 times gently and the rack fell out on the floor. I was so happy, and so pissed at the same time. ||||reg||1
